public with sharing class AccountCountrySync {

    /**
     * Called from the Account after-update trigger.
     *
     * For every account whose BillingCountry changed, updates that account's
     * contacts whose MailingCountry is blank or equal to the account's
     * previous BillingCountry, setting MailingCountry to the new
     * BillingCountry. All other contacts are left untouched.
     *
     * Bulk-safe: exactly one SOQL statement and one DML statement per
     * transaction, scoped to the accounts in the current trigger batch whose
     * BillingCountry actually changed. Queries are never executed inside a
     * loop.
     */
    public static void syncMailingCountry(List<Account> newAccounts, Map<Id, Account> oldMap) {
        // Collect only the accounts in this batch whose BillingCountry changed,
        // mapped to their new BillingCountry.
        Map<Id, String> changedAccounts = new Map<Id, String>();
        for (Account acc : newAccounts) {
            Account oldAccount = oldMap.get(acc.Id);
            if (oldAccount.BillingCountry != acc.BillingCountry) {
                changedAccounts.put(acc.Id, acc.BillingCountry);
            }
        }

        // Accounts whose BillingCountry did not change: do nothing.
        if (changedAccounts.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // One query for the contacts of the affected accounts only.
        // We filter the blank/previous-country rule in Apex below so that each
        // contact is compared against its own account's previous BillingCountry
        // (a global filter on country values could wrongly match contacts of
        // other accounts in the same batch).
        List<Contact> contactsToUpdate = new List<Contact>();
        for (Contact con : [
                SELECT Id, AccountId, MailingCountry
                FROM Contact
                WHERE AccountId IN :changedAccounts.keySet()
        ]) {
            String previousBillingCountry = oldMap.get(con.AccountId).BillingCountry;

            if (con.MailingCountry == null || con.MailingCountry == previousBillingCountry) {
                con.MailingCountry = changedAccounts.get(con.AccountId);
                contactsToUpdate.add(con);
            }
            // Contacts with any other MailingCountry work abroad; leave them alone.
        }

        if (!contactsToUpdate.isEmpty()) {
            update contactsToUpdate;
        }
    }
}
```

**One caveat worth flagging to your MDM team:** if a single account in a batch has hundreds of thousands of contacts, the `update` on those contacts itself could approach the 10,000-row DML limit per transaction. For the normal case (a headquarters move affecting thousands of contacts at most) this code is comfortably within limits. If single accounts routinely carry six-figure contact counts, this should be moved to an async path (e.g., a `Queueable` with `Database.Stateful` chaining or a batch job) keyed off the same trigger — happy to provide that variant if needed.

